The AI Act does not prescribe a universal list of eight contract clauses for deployers. Procurement must still secure the information and assistance that lawful operation actually requires.

**Possible review fields (implementation choices rather than a statutory clause list):**
- intended purpose, system and model version, performance limits, and instructions for use under Article 13;
- evidence of the provider role, CE marking, and EU declaration of conformity where applicable;
- quality, bias, robustness, and security testing proportionate to the risk;
- notification and documentation of material updates;
- access to logs and information the deployer needs for Article 26 and data-protection compliance;
- support concerning risks, incidents, data-subject rights, authority requests, exit arrangements, and deletion of data.

Reporting frequency, response periods, audit rights, liability, contractual penalties, language, governing law, and jurisdiction are risk-based procurement and negotiation points. Their suitability depends on the market position, sector, and system. In an incident, XY GmbH follows Article 26(5); the provider performs its reporting duty under Article 73. Identify the competent national or sectoral market-surveillance authority from the current implementation framework.
